# Signing in

Out of the box, `howdy_auth` signs people in with a token sent to their
email address:

1. The visitor gives their address. Your app emails them a token.
2. They paste the token into the page.
3. The app exchanges the token for a session.

There is no password to choose, forget or leak, and owning the inbox is the
proof. [Passwords](/docs/auth/passwords), [passkeys](/docs/auth/passkeys)
and [social providers](/docs/auth/providers) can be added as other ways in.
An app that only uses providers can leave email tokens out altogether, with
`auth.new_without_email`.

Registration works the same way, and the account is only created once the
token has been exchanged, so every account has a working address.
Registration is off until you call `auth.allow_registration`.

## Sending the email

The `deliver` function you give `auth.new` is called with an
`auth.Delivery`: the `email` to write to, the `token`, and a `purpose`.

The quickest way to write one is `howdy/auth/emails`, which has a
ready-made email for every purpose, sent with
[Howdy Mail](/docs/mail/overview):

```gleam
import howdy/auth
import howdy/auth/emails
import howdy/mail
import howdy/mail/smtp

let assert Ok(config) = smtp.from_env()
let mailer =
  mail.mailer(smtp.adapter(config))
  |> mail.default_from(mail.named("Acme", "hello@acme.test"))
let auth_emails = emails.new(mailer, app_name: "Acme")

let assert Ok(identity) =
  auth.new(repo: db, origin:, deliver: emails.deliver(auth_emails))
```

Each email is tagged `auth.<purpose>`, such as `auth.sign_in`, and
`emails.previews(auth_emails)` gives the admin area a
[preview](/docs/mail/development) of every one.
`emails.with_template(auth_emails, for: auth.SignIn, build: ...)` replaces
one with your own.

You can also write `deliver` yourself:

```gleam
import gleam/result
import howdy/auth
import howdy/auth/secret
import howdy/mail

pub fn send_email(mailer: mail.Mailer, delivery: auth.Delivery) {
  let subject = case delivery.purpose {
    auth.SignIn -> "Your sign-in token"
    auth.Registration -> "Confirm your new account"
    auth.AlreadyRegistered -> "You already have an account"
    auth.EmailChange -> "Confirm your new email address"
    auth.EmailChangeApproval -> "Approve changing your email address"
    auth.EmailChanged -> "Your email address was changed"
    auth.PasswordChanged -> "Your password was changed"
  }
  mail.message()
  |> mail.to([mail.address(delivery.email)])
  |> mail.subject(subject)
  |> mail.text(
    "Paste this token into the page: " <> secret.reveal(delivery.token),
  )
  |> mail.send(mailer, _)
  |> result.replace(Nil)
  |> result.replace_error(Nil)
}
```

Give `auth.new` the function `send_email(mailer, _)`.

`AlreadyRegistered` arrives when somebody registers an address that has an
account. The token it carries signs them in, so say that, instead of
inviting them to register again. Whoever made the request sees the same
answer in every case, so only the owner of the inbox learns anything.

The other four purposes come from [account changes](/docs/auth/account)
and [password changes](/docs/auth/passwords):

- `EmailChange` and `EmailChangeApproval` carry tokens that confirm or
  approve a new address. They cannot be used to sign in.
- `EmailChanged` and `PasswordChanged` are notices, and their token is
  empty. Tell a reader who did not make the change what to do: contact you,
  or reset their password from a fresh email sign-in.

The token is a `secret.Secret`, which does not print its contents when the
record around it is logged. Call `secret.reveal` only where the email is
written. Do not log tokens, and do not put them in links: they are exchanged
by `POST`, never from a URL.

A token is random, works once, and lasts ten minutes. Returning `Error(Nil)`
from `deliver` cancels it. Exchanging a token spends it even when the
exchange then fails, for instance because the user is suspended.

While you develop, print the token instead of sending it:

```gleam
fn(delivery: auth.Delivery) {
  io.println(delivery.email <> ": " <> secret.reveal(delivery.token))
  Ok(Nil)
}
```

## Asking twice

A request for a token that finds a usable one already waiting for that
address sends nothing, and still reports success, because there is already
one in the inbox. A new request never cancels tokens sent before it: the
three most recent stay valid.

That is what stops a stranger from flooding somebody's inbox, or from
locking its owner out by asking again just before they paste. It holds
however many machines the stranger asks from, because the limit belongs to
the inbox and not to the asker. The numbers are part of the
[policy](/docs/auth/sessions).

## The JSON API

`routes.api(identity, at: "/api/auth")` mounts these, relative to the mount
path:

- `POST /register` with `{"email":"…"}` sends a registration token.
  Answers `202`.
- `POST /login` with `{"email":"…"}` sends a sign-in token. Answers `202`
  whether or not the address has an account.
- `POST /session` with `{"token":"…"}` exchanges a token. Answers with the
  user as JSON, and sets the session cookie.
- `POST /token` with `{"token":"…"}` exchanges a token for a bearer token:
  `access_token`, `token_type`, `expires_in` and `user`.
- `GET /me` answers with the signed-in user.
- `GET /sessions` lists the caller's sessions.
- `POST /sessions/revoke` with `{"id":"…"}` ends one of them. Answers
  `204`.
- `POST /logout` with a JSON body such as `{}` ends this session. Answers
  `204`.

With [passwords](/docs/auth/passwords) on there are more under
`/password`, and [passkeys](/docs/auth/passkeys),
[second factors](/docs/auth/mfa), [several accounts](/docs/auth/sessions)
and [account changes](/docs/auth/account) each add their own. The endpoints that take an `email` also take an optional
`"group"`: see [Groups](/docs/auth/groups).

A user with a [second factor](/docs/auth/mfa) does not get a session from
`/session` or `/token` straight away. The answer is `202` with
`{"mfa_required": true}`, and the code is checked at `/mfa/verify` or
`/mfa/token`.

The user is `id`, `email`, `group_id`, and `created_at` and `updated_at` as
RFC 3339 strings. Errors have the usual
[Howdy shape](/docs/controllers/services).

Every `POST` must have the content type `application/json`. Responses carry
`cache-control: no-store`. Endpoints that send email, exchange tokens or
check passwords allow each client 30 requests a minute, and the signed-in
ones allow 300; behind a proxy, see
[Behind a proxy](/docs/auth/administration).

## From a browser

Use the cookie endpoints. The cookie is `HttpOnly`, so scripts on the page
cannot read it, and it is sent with every request to your app:

```javascript
await fetch('/api/auth/login', {
  method: 'POST',
  headers: {'Content-Type': 'application/json'},
  body: JSON.stringify({email}),
});

// Once the visitor has pasted the token:
const response = await fetch('/api/auth/session', {
  method: 'POST',
  credentials: 'same-origin',
  headers: {'Content-Type': 'application/json'},
  body: JSON.stringify({token}),
});
```

`POST /session` requires the `origin` header to match the origin you
configured, which a browser on your own site sends without being asked.
Avoid `/token` in a browser: a bearer token kept in JavaScript storage can
be read by any script on the page.

The cookie is named `__Host-howdy_session` on HTTPS and `howdy_dev_session`
on a loopback origin; `auth.cookie_name(identity)` tells you which. It is
`Secure`, `HttpOnly`, `SameSite=Lax` and scoped to `/`.

## From other clients

A native app or a script exchanges at `/token`, and sends the result with
each request:

```
Authorization: Bearer <access_token>
```

These clients need not send `origin`. A request whose `origin` is present
and wrong is refused, as is one carrying both a cookie and a bearer token.

## Without HTTP

Every endpoint is a thin layer over a function that returns a
[`service.Result`](/docs/controllers/services), with no HTML, cookies or
redirects. Use them to build server-rendered forms, or another transport
altogether:

- `auth.request_token_from(identity, email, auth.Login, client)`, or
  `auth.Register`
- `auth.exchange_from(identity, token, client)` returns an `auth.Session`:
  the `user`, the secret `token`, and `expires_at`
- `auth.authenticate_from(identity, token, client)` returns the
  `user.Principal` for a session token
- `auth.sessions`, `auth.revoke_session` and `auth.logout`

With [multi-factor authentication](/docs/auth/mfa) on, use
`auth.exchange_step` instead of `auth.exchange`. It says whether the user is
signed in, or must pass a second factor first.

`client` is a string naming where the request came from, normally the IP
address. It is used for throttling and recorded in the audit trail. The
shorter names `request_token`, `exchange` and `authenticate` leave it out,
and share one bucket between all callers.

A transport of your own is responsible for what the routes did for you:
checking `origin` on cookie writes with `auth.check_origin`, setting the
cookie, `no-store` on responses, and limiting how fast clients can call.
